```
This means if `self.call` is a class, we skip it and check `self._unwrapped_call`.
If `self._unwrapped_call` is also a class, we skip it and return `False`.
This matches the original behavior for classes, but allows `self.call` to be an async function that wraps a class.

Wait, if `self.call` is a class, `inspect.isroutine(self.call)` is `False`.
Then `inspect.isclass(self.call)` is `True`, so we `continue`.
Then we check `self._unwrapped_call`.
If `self._unwrapped_call` is also a class, we `continue` and return `False`.
This is correct.

But what if `self.call` is a class and it has an async `__call__`?
The original code:
`inspect.isroutine(self._unwrapped_call)` is `False`.
`inspect.isclass(self._unwrapped_call)` is `True`.
Returns `False`.
So it didn't even check `__call__` if it was a class.

Wait, if `self._unwrapped_call` is a class, `is_coroutine_callable` returns `False`.
But if `self._unwrapped_call` is an instance of a class, it checks `__call__`.

